                    coach_default:          `Decision recorded.`,
                    coach_r1_social:        `<strong>Ranking on social return is the discipline.</strong> The economic rate of return captures time savings, consumer surplus and externalities — the bankable IRR captures only cash to the investor. A project can be socially excellent and financially unviable; that gap is exactly what a subsidy is meant to bridge. Lead with social value and use bankability as the second filter.`,
                    coach_r1_bankability:   `<strong>Ranking by IRR alone reproduces Peru’s problem.</strong> The most bankable project — the toll road serving the wealthiest sub-region — is also the one private capital would have built anyway. Order by bankability and you will pour public money where it adds the least, and starve the province that needs it most.`,
                    coach_r1_cost:          `<strong>Cheapest-first stretches the budget but not the social return.</strong> Cost is a constraint, not a ranking criterion. A cheap, low-social project funded ahead of an expensive, high-social one is a false economy. Rank on what the corridor is <em>for</em>.`,
                    coach_r1_independent:   `<strong>Regulating on the independent number is how you break the cycle.</strong> The +30% gap is the over-optimism marker. Set the toll and the investor’s IRR on credible demand and a year-3 traffic miss no longer triggers a renegotiation — the concessionaire was never promised a number it could not hit.`,
                    coach_r1_sponsor:       `<strong>You set the toll and the IRR on a forecast 30% above the independent study.</strong> In year 3, traffic comes in at the independent number, revenue misses, and the concessionaire files for renegotiation — exactly the cycle ARIC was created to stop. Whose forecast did you regulate on, and what did you do with the 30% gap?`,
                    coach_r1_average:       `<strong>Splitting the difference still bakes in 15% of over-optimism.</strong> The independent study is the disciplined number, not a bargaining position. Averaging it with the sponsor’s wish-list leaves a forecast the road still cannot hit — and a smaller, but real, renegotiation seed.`,
                    coach_r1_additionality: `<strong>That is the purpose of subsidy.</strong> Public co-financing earns its place where it changes the outcome — the socially valuable bridge that private capital will not build at 8.1% IRR. Pour it into the already-bankable road and you are subsidising a deal that would have closed anyway.`,
                    coach_r2_bridgestarved: `<strong>You deferred the highest-social-return project.</strong> The isolated province — 22% economic return — got nothing, while public money went to corridors private capital would have built. The minister’s question lands: what was the co-financing <em>for</em>, if not the project that only exists with it?`,
                    coach_r2_bankableonly:  `<strong>You funded the bankable road and deferred the social bridge.</strong> Aggregate IRR looks great. Now the finance minister asks why public money went to the project private capital would have built anyway — and why the province with the highest social return got nothing. This is the additionality failure the simulation is about.`,
                    coach_r2_thinspread:    `<strong>You gave every project a slice so nobody loses.</strong> But three are now underfunded below their viability threshold and none reaches financial close. Equal isn’t optimal — political even-handedness bought you four stalled projects instead of two that work.`,
                    coach_r2_disciplined:   `<strong>That is portfolio discipline.</strong> You funded the high-social-return bridge fully and backed solid projects without trying to do everything — and you had the discipline to cut or defer the bankable road that private capital would have built anyway. The envelope went where it adds the most.`,
                    coach_r3_capped:        `<strong>A capped, priced guarantee is additionality done right.</strong> It makes the sub-hurdle bridge investable without overpaying the investor, and the Treasury’s downside is valued and bounded — on the balance sheet, not hidden off it. That is how you make the isolated province bankable without writing a blank cheque.`,
                    coach_r3_uncapped:      `<strong>Three guarantees, none capped.</strong> You made every project investable — by writing the Treasury a blank cheque worth maybe S/ 600M in present value if demand disappoints. That liability is off your budget line but very much on the country’s balance sheet. Where is the cap, and who priced the downside?`,
                    coach_r3_nomrg:         `<strong>No guarantee at all leaves the bridge stranded.</strong> The isolated-province project sits below the 10.5% hurdle; with no support it will not reach financial close. The discipline is not “no guarantees” — it is a capped, priced guarantee exactly where additionality justifies it.`,
                    coach_r3_hurdle:        `<strong>You raised the toll until the IRR cleared 10.5%.</strong> Clean — except usage falls ~18% because users reroute, the social benefit you justified the project on evaporates, and a regional governor is on the news calling it a tax on the poor. Tariff regulation is a usage-and-legitimacy problem, not just a cash-flow one.`,
                    coach_r3_lowtariff:     `<strong>A giveaway tariff erodes investment, not just cost recovery.</strong> Price below efficient cost and the concession cannot fund maintenance — the asset degrades and the next renegotiation is about underfunding, not over-optimism. Affordability matters; so does a fair return.`,
                    coach_r3_riskinvestor:  `<strong>Dumping all demand risk on the investor looks Treasury-safe.</strong> But a party that cannot bear demand risk prices it back into a higher tariff — or files for renegotiation the moment traffic disappoints. Assign each risk to whoever manages it best; that is what a capped, shared band is for.`,
                    coach_r3_indextraffic:  `<strong>Indexing the tariff to the sponsor’s forecast bakes in the over-optimism.</strong> If demand misses, the rule itself raises the toll to defend the projected revenue — the renegotiation written into the contract. Index to CPI with a performance review, not to a number the road cannot hit.`,
                    coach_r3_longplaza:     `<strong>A very long concession lowers the tariff but stretches the risk window.</strong> Thirty years of demand uncertainty is thirty years in which the forecast can be wrong and the contract can be reopened. Match the term to the asset life and the credibility of the demand study, not to the lowest possible toll.`,
                    coach_r3_clean:         `<strong>That is a renegotiation-resistant structure.</strong> A balanced, affordable tariff; demand risk shared in a capped band; a valued, capped guarantee; and CPI indexation with a performance review. Nothing here promises the investor a number the corridor cannot deliver — which is precisely why it will not return for amendment.`,
                    coach_r4_balanced:      `<strong>Leading with the balanced portfolio is the defensible memo.</strong> Social value with additionality, bounded exposure, renegotiation-resistant contracts — that is the story both the lender and the minister can act on. You are not selling a single KPI; you are showing the corridor was regulated, not just financed.`,
                    coach_r4_investment:    `<strong>Leading with “private investment mobilised” invites the obvious probe.</strong> Mobilised into what? If the answer is the projects private capital would have built anyway, you have maximised near-term investment and recreated the bailout cycle. The minister wants additionality, not a deal-flow number.`,
                    coach_r4_harmony:       `<strong>“No region got nothing” is the thin-spread defence.</strong> Political even-handedness is not a regulatory standard. The lender will ask which projects actually reach financial close — and an evenly sliced envelope where none does is the worst of both worlds.`,
                    coach_r4_additionality: `<strong>That is the answer the minister is looking for.</strong> Public money funded exactly what private capital would not build alone, the forecast was disciplined, and the guarantees are capped and priced. You can name what the subsidy bought, what it cost, and why none of these contracts is likely to return for renegotiation.`,
                    coach_r4_leverage:      `<strong>“They would have come anyway” is the additionality problem stated out loud.</strong> If investors would have built it without you, the public money added nothing but speed — and you spent a scarce envelope to accelerate deals that did not need it. That is not what concessional finance is for.`,
                    coach_r4_blame:         `<strong>Blaming the sponsor’s forecast is the regulator abdicating its job.</strong> You set the tariff and the IRR; choosing which forecast to regulate on <em>was</em> the decision. If it returns for renegotiation, the contract you signed is the evidence — not the sponsor’s optimism.`,

